WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:05.458
[MUSIC].

00:00:05.458 --> 00:00:10.560
There are over 70 million middle children
in the United States right now.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.370
We're talking about over 70 million
Americans with both older and

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.270
younger siblings.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:18.900
But, if you watch a lot of movies and
television.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:19.745
And I do.

00:00:19.745 --> 00:00:21.040
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.260
&gt;&gt; You'd think all middle children are
exactly the same.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.290
Take for example, Jan Brady of the Brady
Bunch,

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.570
always angrily complaining about her
perfect sister, Marcia.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:36.810
Or Malcolm in the Middle, who's parents
always forget to pick him up from school.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:40.060
Ron Weasley,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.384
lost in a sea of red-haired siblings,
drowning in oversized hand-me-downs.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:51.200
Or sniveling Edith from Downton Abbey
[LAUGH],

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.290
always angry, coveting everything our
sisters achieve.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:00.480
Again and again, middle children are
depicted as neglected ignored,

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.940
low in self-esteem, starving for
attention.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.770
And this has to come from some basis in
reality.

00:01:07.792 --> 00:01:11.900
After all, our country's most famous
middle child right now would have to be

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.715
Kim Kardashian, and who is more starving
for attention than her?

00:01:15.715 --> 00:01:18.782
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:01:18.782 --> 00:01:22.292
&gt;&gt; But, I'm a middle child and both my
parents are middle children.

00:01:22.292 --> 00:01:25.860
So, I've always been fascinated at where
this perception of middle children

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:26.419
comes from.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:32.148
Society hands us this view of birth order,
this spectrum.

00:01:32.148 --> 00:01:36.050
You have dominant eldest children on one
end and

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.150
beloved youngest children on the other.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:41.947
Us, middle children, are just lost
somewhere in between.

00:01:41.947 --> 00:01:45.810
But the more I thought about it, the more
wrong I've realized that world view is.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.670
Today, we'll start off by looking at birth
order research,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.400
to understand where this perception of
middle children comes from.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.870
Then, we'll separately analyze the role of
older sibling and younger sibling.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.214
Before finally, bringing it all together
to try to define what

00:02:01.214 --> 00:02:04.819
it means to live from the middle, whether
you have siblings or not.

00:02:04.819 --> 00:02:07.141
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:02:07.141 --> 00:02:08.790
So, let's start with the data.

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:11.788
Researchers have done a great job
explaining the characteristics of

00:02:11.788 --> 00:02:13.750
firstborns and lastborns.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.720
So, who here is the oldest child in their
family?

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:17.330
A firstborn?

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.660
Wow.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.402
&gt;&gt; So, you guys have it made.

00:02:21.402 --> 00:02:24.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You will, you'll earn 1%

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.810
more income than your younger siblings and
have fewer illnesses too.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.640
Over 60% of CEO's, Ivy League graduates
and

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.090
US Presidents are first born in their
family.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.440
And as if this wasn't all enough.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.481
Statistically speaking,

00:02:38.481 --> 00:02:41.115
your parents take way more photos of you
than your younger siblings.

00:02:41.115 --> 00:02:42.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.375
&gt;&gt; So, you got a great head start in the
Instagram game.

00:02:44.375 --> 00:02:46.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.766
&gt;&gt; Now, who here is the youngest child in
their family, the baby?

00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:50.461
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:52.580
You guys have it pretty good too, I'd say.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.590
You got more of lenient punishments for

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.615
the same behavior compared to your
siblings.

00:02:57.615 --> 00:02:58.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.260
&gt;&gt; You are more likely to protest for more
positive social change.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.845
And you have the highest self-esteem of
any birth order.

00:03:04.845 --> 00:03:05.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.935
&gt;&gt; So congratulations, although apparently
you guys do not need the ego boost.

00:03:09.935 --> 00:03:12.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.329
&gt;&gt; So, where does that leave us middles?

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:17.046
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:03:17.046 --> 00:03:19.362
A study out of Cornell talked to mothers
and

00:03:19.362 --> 00:03:23.863
found that regardless of how many children
they have, mothers tend to turn to their

00:03:23.863 --> 00:03:28.100
firstborn child in moments of crisis when
they need advice.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:32.050
But they feel the most emotionally
connected to their youngest child.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.810
Middle children just didn't factor in
heavily to their answer to either

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:35.345
question.

00:03:35.345 --> 00:03:36.520
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.930
&gt;&gt; Professor Catherine Salmon ran a
similar series of studies.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:45.150
In one, she asked undergraduates, who of
all people you know are you closest to?

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.520
What one person?

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.249
64% of firstborn children and

00:03:50.249 --> 00:03:54.030
39% of lastborn children named a parent as
that one person.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:55.300
Only 10% of middle children did.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:59.430
Middle children have a fundamentally
different attachment style to their

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.220
parents.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.760
And it's lead researchers to coin the term
Middle Child Syndrome, which refers to

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:08.250
the tendency of middle children to
struggle to find a role for themselves in

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.390
their family, and as a result, a role for
themselves in the world.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.140
Middle children have the lowest
self-esteem of any birth order,

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:19.165
although I'd imagine having a syndrome
named after us isn't helping things.

00:04:19.165 --> 00:04:23.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.270
&gt;&gt; Why do I wanna speak out on behalf of
middle children?

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.860
Because I think part of the reason Middle
Child Syndrome exists

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:32.109
is because it's not something we talk
about or think about.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.210
Not part of any conversation.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.200
I've had discussions with about a dozen
middle children in the last few weeks.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.433
And I've been struck by how difficult it
is for us to define our role in our

00:04:41.433 --> 00:04:44.039
family, and how it shaped us, even though
we know it did.

00:04:44.039 --> 00:04:47.429
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:04:47.429 --> 00:04:49.060
It comes back to this fundamental
question.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.850
What does it mean to live from the middle?

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:56.605
And I think to understand that, we first
need to understand separately, the role of

00:04:56.605 --> 00:05:00.099
older sibling and a younger sibling,
because they are very, very different.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:01.772
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:05:01.772 --> 00:05:04.920
So, I grew up looking up to my older
brother, Josh.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.900
He's two years older than me, and a
classic firstborn.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.610
He's ambitious, he's driven,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.705
he's high-achieving, responsible, a bit
neurotic.

00:05:14.705 --> 00:05:16.690
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:17.410
&gt;&gt; As a short cut for

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:21.170
my own decision making process, I used to
just copy the things he did.

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:23.355
But trying to be not too Jan Brady about
it.

00:05:23.355 --> 00:05:25.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.140
&gt;&gt; I tried to define myself by doing the
same things as him, but

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.550
with my own spin, differently, hopefully
better.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.270
If he played tennis, I played tennis.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.740
If he did highschool newspaper, I did too.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.155
Sometimes, we even dress the same.

00:05:36.155 --> 00:05:38.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.960
&gt;&gt; Having an older sibling gives you
something to prove.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.480
Someone to emulate.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:43.531
But differentiate from.

00:05:43.531 --> 00:05:45.783
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:05:45.783 --> 00:05:49.630
On the other hand, being an older brother
to Brett, who's three years younger,

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:50.990
gave me someone to approve.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:51.880
Someone to mentor.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:56.280
Like many youngest children, Brett is
artistic and creative.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.748
He is an inspiring graphic designer.

00:05:57.748 --> 00:05:59.373
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:05:59.373 --> 00:06:02.737
My mom would often want me to spend more
time with Brett growing up, so

00:06:02.737 --> 00:06:06.660
I played video games with him, never
letting him win of course.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:10.900
I teach him how to use a computer program
or help him with his Math homework.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.441
Sometimes, pretending to be annoyed, but
always so

00:06:13.441 --> 00:06:16.326
happy that someone needed my help and was
willing to take it.

00:06:16.326 --> 00:06:18.902
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:06:18.902 --> 00:06:22.279
So, I think in my relationship with Josh,
my relationship with Brett and

00:06:22.279 --> 00:06:23.430
how different they are.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:27.000
And I think of this spectrum, this world
view we're given about birth order.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.700
Ambitious Josh on one end, creative Brett
on the other,

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.830
me supposedly lost somewhere in between.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.258
But that wasn't my experience as a middle
child.

00:06:37.258 --> 00:06:38.466
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:06:38.466 --> 00:06:42.050
Being a middle child isn't about being
lost between two roles.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.995
It's about being firmly planted,

00:06:43.995 --> 00:06:46.530
planted on both ends of the spectrum at
the same time.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.460
To have experienced being older and
younger.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.880
To know what it feels like to be the top
dog and the underdog.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.832
And when you think about that perception
of being the middle child,

00:06:56.832 --> 00:06:59.341
it unlocks amazing and unique qualities.

00:06:59.341 --> 00:07:00.734
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:04.115
Middle children, from a young age,
understand though,

00:07:04.115 --> 00:07:08.257
what it feels like to be in a role of
authority and a role of inferiority.

00:07:08.257 --> 00:07:11.824
And because of that, research has shown
that middle children are great

00:07:11.824 --> 00:07:14.689
negotiators, great mediators, great peace
keepers.

00:07:14.689 --> 00:07:15.989
They're comfortable with ambiguity.

00:07:15.989 --> 00:07:17.708
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:07:17.708 --> 00:07:21.945
Similarly, middle children don't have the
responsibility of the first born or

00:07:21.945 --> 00:07:25.820
the attention of the last born, so they're
free to chart their own path.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.130
They measure high in measures of
independence.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:34.200
An NYU study compared middle born CEOs to
firstborn CEOs, and

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:38.050
found that middle born CEOs tend to be
change makers.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.800
They're used to setting their own
expectations so,

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.000
they're not afraid to do things
differently to experiment.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.185
This may explain Kim Kardashian's unique
approach to business.

00:07:47.185 --> 00:07:52.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So you,

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.240
you may be thinking, kind of, these are
qualities we all can relate to.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.550
And, I think you're right.

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:02.870
There are only 70 million middle children
in the United States, but

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:06.133
there's 300 million of us who are used to
living from the middle,

00:08:06.133 --> 00:08:08.060
because that's what we're all doing.

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:09.895
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:08:09.895 --> 00:08:14.472
At business schools, we're taught to value
traits that come naturally to firstborns.

00:08:14.472 --> 00:08:16.400
To wanna be at the head of the pack.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.830
To report to no one.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.020
To give a view from the top.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:21.835
But life isn't business school.

00:08:21.835 --> 00:08:23.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:26.910
&gt;&gt; We're not either first years, who have
endless questions, or second years,

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:27.845
who know everything.

00:08:27.845 --> 00:08:29.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:31.170
&gt;&gt; We're somewhere in between.

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:34.280
We're between our board and our employees.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.750
Between our managers and our reports.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.940
We can be between our family and our
career.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:41.230
Or our ego and our insecurity.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:45.460
Instead of aspiring to make power plays,
or

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:50.700
step on anyone who gets in our way, we can
aspire to take root firmly in the middle.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:55.052
Because being a middle means having the
humility to take advice from others, but

00:08:55.052 --> 00:08:58.390
the confidence to know you have a voice,
that you have worth.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:01.478
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:09:01.478 --> 00:09:06.702
A few years ago, my dad underwent an
extensive brain surgery.

00:09:06.702 --> 00:09:10.725
It was the scariest day of my life, but in
his road to full recovery,

00:09:10.725 --> 00:09:13.064
it brought my family closer together.

00:09:13.064 --> 00:09:14.783
This picture was taken a few months later.

00:09:14.783 --> 00:09:16.596
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:09:16.596 --> 00:09:18.905
My dad had spent a few nights in the
hospital afterwards and

00:09:18.905 --> 00:09:20.332
preferred to not spend them alone.

00:09:20.332 --> 00:09:23.106
So, I volunteered to camp out in the chair
next to his bed.

00:09:23.106 --> 00:09:27.870
Napping, chatting, watching TV.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:29.610
He'd ask for help getting up out of bed.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.707
And then, a few minutes later, give me
advice,

00:09:31.707 --> 00:09:34.896
remind me to take care of my mom the next
few weeks cuz it'd be tough on her.

00:09:34.896 --> 00:09:36.429
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:09:36.429 --> 00:09:39.600
I would interrogate a doctor, on his
behalf.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.050
Only an hour after after having my dad
proofread an email for

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:44.420
me that I might write my boss.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:48.380
The nights were filled with these moments
where our parent-child

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:52.499
relationship was completely inverted, yet
somehow it felt exactly the same.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:57.050
Each of us sometimes acting older,
sometimes acting younger, but

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.158
always feeling comfortable in either role.

00:09:59.158 --> 00:10:01.805
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:10:01.805 --> 00:10:05.539
I think those nights at [UNKNOWN] General
Hospital with my dad at 4 a.m were two

00:10:05.539 --> 00:10:07.540
middle children at their best.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.500
Looking up and looking down at the very
same time.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:15.942
And it's moments like these that make me
realize the view from the middle,

00:10:15.942 --> 00:10:16.982
it's not so bad.

00:10:16.982 --> 00:10:18.212
[BLANK_AUDIO].

00:10:18.212 --> 00:10:21.477
Thank you.

00:10:21.477 --> 00:10:22.366
[APPLAUSE].

00:10:22.366 --> 00:10:26.679
[MUSIC].

